# Project Zomboid B42 modding: workflow

Game is 42.21. Docs live in `docs/` (start at `docs/README.md`; read the topic file before designing or debugging). The local mods still have many unfound bugs and poor performance: never treat their code as a correct example without checking vanilla. Update the docs when you confirm or disprove something.

## Before writing code
1. Identify the exact object: map tile (`IsoObject`), player-built (`IsoThumpable` from a B42 entity script), moveable item, or script item.
2. Grep vanilla (`C:\Program Files (x86)\Steam\steamapps\common\ProjectZomboid\media\lua`, `media\scripts\generated`) before claiming a mechanic exists or quoting a number.
3. Not in vanilla Lua means it's Java (`projectzomboid.jar`); Lua can't change rendering, collision, animal AI, physics, or most of the world context menu.
4. Something broken or slow? Read the log first (`C:\Users\alpar\Zomboid\console.txt`, or ErrorMonitor output once it exists); group exceptions by message.
5. Implement the user's spec literally; no extra steps, immunities or categories.

## While coding
1. Copy an existing vanilla call site for every API; a signature existing is not proof of correct use.
2. Never edit `steamapps\workshop\content\108600\...`; make a local patch mod with its own mod id.
3. Edit files with the editor tools, never PowerShell `Get-Content | Set-Content`.
4. No `pcall` to silence errors. Check the cost of every frequent event you register (`docs/performance-debugging.md`).

## Before saying "done"
1. Top-level `require`/`derive`/globals resolve in the same or an earlier folder (`shared` → `client` → `server`).
2. Every `Base.X` exists in vanilla `scripts/generated/items` or the mod's scripts; every translation key is in a loaded `.json`.
3. Javap, luaparser and mock tests prove syntax/signatures only. Say what was tested in game and what wasn't.

## Enabling and testing
1. A save loads `Zomboid\Saves\<Mode>\<save>\mods.txt`; `mods\default.txt` and the main-menu Mods screen don't affect existing saves.
2. Edit `mods.txt` only when `Get-Process ProjectZomboid64 -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue` returns nothing; back it up first.
3. Don't launch the game; ask the user. Afterwards confirm the log's line 1 is newer than your edits, `loading <ModId>` appears, and your files raise no errors.
4. Ask before touching save data beyond `mods.txt`.

## Communicating
1. When the user says it doesn't work, your model is wrong; gather evidence instead of arguing.
2. The thing the user asked for stays first, even when scope widens.
